Subject: The runtime records that an object with the exotic `== null` behaviour exists

What this does
  - `JSRuntime` gets `aot_htmldda`, set by `JS_SetIsHTMLDDA`, the only place the object bit is set, which also moves the
    prototype cache epoch.
  - The layout export carries the field's offset, so the unit's layout hash changes with it.

Why we need it
  A compiled nullish test looks at the tag alone while no such object exists. The flag is what the unit's entry tests; the
  epoch move makes a body that is running leave at its next epoch test, and the next entry refuses while the flag is set.

  Soundness: the bit is set only by a host call on an object that already exists. No body runs while that call runs
  (one thread); every body suspended below it resumes through a call return, where the epoch test follows, and a body
  entered later reads the flag at its entry. A body that makes no call cannot hold the object before the flag is set
  and still see it afterwards, since the call that sets the flag is not inside it. A getter, a setter, a proxy trap, a
  toString or valueOf is a call; an array element, a property slot or a closed variable read is not a way for a new
  value to arrive: the value was in the heap before, and the flag is set before the next epoch test of any body
  holding it.

Functions touched
  `JS_SetIsHTMLDDA`, `js_aot_gen_layout`.

No.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -762,0 +763,3 @@
+    /* Set once an object with the exotic `== null` behaviour exists. A compiled body tests nullish by
+       tag alone only while it is clear; setting it moves the cache epoch. */
+    uint8_t aot_htmldda;
@@ -17709,0 +17713,4 @@
+    ctx->rt->aot_htmldda = 1;
+#if JS_ENABLE_IC
+    ic_watchpoint_bump(ctx->rt);
+#endif
@@ -62484,0 +62492 @@
+    out->off_rt_htmldda = (uint32_t)offsetof(JSRuntime, aot_htmldda);
--- a/quickjs-aot-rt.h
+++ b/quickjs-aot-rt.h
@@ -75,0 +76 @@
+    uint32_t off_rt_htmldda;
